WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.500
Bug bounties and vulnerability disclosure programs can create strong public evidence, but only when the researcher respects the program's rules.

2
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.480
The first skill is reading the policy carefully.

3
00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:19.500
Scope, prohibited techniques, data handling expectations, disclosure timing, and report format all matter.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:25.120
A target listed in a program is not an invitation to test everything related to the organization.

5
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.340
The allowed work is whatever the policy authorizes.

6
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.340
A strong report is concise, reproducible, and restrained.

7
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:41.040
It identifies the affected asset, explains the weakness, describes business or user impact, and provides safe evidence.

8
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.860
It also states what you did not do.

9
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:52.280
For example, you can explain that you stopped after demonstrating access to a synthetic record, or that you avoided collecting unrelated data.

10
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:56.540
This helps a triager understand both the vulnerability and your judgment.

11
00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:03.940
Duplicate reports, informational findings, and non-awarded submissions are not failures if they are handled professionally.

12
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:10.840
They can still teach you how programs classify impact, how to improve evidence, and where your assumptions were too broad.

13
00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:16.940
In a portfolio, focus less on payout totals and more on the kind of reasoning the work demonstrates.

14
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:23.520
A small valid finding with a thoughtful write-up may say more about your readiness than a dramatic number without context.

15
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.200
Public disclosure requires care.

16
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:30.540
Some programs permit public summaries only after resolution.

17
00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:35.840
Others require approval before publication or prohibit sharing details entirely.

18
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:41.860
Platform codes of conduct may also restrict behavior, communication, and confidentiality.

19
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.400
Respect those terms.

20
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:53.980
A private report can still support your career if you summarize it safely, list general vulnerability classes, or describe process lessons without exposing restricted information.

21
00:01:54.260 --> 00:02:04.100
The best bounty evidence shows a full professional cycle: authorized testing, careful validation, clear reporting, responsive communication, and responsible closure.

22
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.720
That cycle is what employers and peers want to see.

23
00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:11.500
The technical bug matters, but so does the way you handled it.

24
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.480
Offensive-security proficiency includes being the kind of researcher a program can work with again.

25
00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:20.840
When you summarize this work publicly, keep the evidence professional.

26
00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:29.260
Acknowledge the program's rules, avoid implying endorsement unless you have it, and describe the security lesson rather than private operational detail.

27
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:40.400
If only a high-level summary is allowed, that can still demonstrate process maturity, especially when paired with other artifacts that show deeper technical reasoning and careful restraint.
